import Mathlib.Data.Nat.PrimeFin

/-!
# MSE 698186: positive blocks in a multiplicative sign sequence

Source: https://math.stackexchange.com/questions/698186
Author of the question: Dan Brumleve.

This states the general conjecture at the beginning of the source and in its
question 1: for every infinite set S of primes and every natural block length k,
the completely multiplicative sign sequence with value -1 on the primes in S
and +1 on the other primes has infinitely many blocks of k consecutive +1s.

The generated sequence is defined by its prime-factor product, with factors
counted with multiplicity. This determines the required sequence on positive
integers; only positive starting points occur in the conclusion. No assertion
about the unused value at zero is needed. The source's separate questions about
the Liouville function (S equal to all primes) are not asserted here.
-/

namespace OMA.S_mse_698186

/-- The completely multiplicative sign sequence generated by S, on positive n. -/
noncomputable def generated (S : Set ℕ) (n : ℕ) : ℤ := by
  classical
  exact (n.primeFactorsList.map fun p => if p ∈ S then (-1 : ℤ) else 1).prod

/-- Every infinite negative-prime set generates infinitely many positive blocks
of every natural length. -/
theorem main :
    ∀ S : Set ℕ, (∀ p ∈ S, p.Prime) → S.Infinite →
      ∀ k : ℕ, {n : ℕ | 0 < n ∧ ∀ i : ℕ, i < k → generated S (n + i) = 1}.Infinite := by
  sorry

end OMA.S_mse_698186
